Unescape the delimiter in a \cREGEXc address.
In an address regex with a custom delimiter, "\c" stands for the
delimiter character c, as it does in the s command, but the address
parser left the backslash in place and handed "\c" to the regex
compiler. Where "\c" has a meaning of its own the address matched the
wrong lines: "\|a\|b|p" was an alternation in a basic regex instead of
matching "a|b", and in an extended regex it matched only a literal "|"
instead of alternating. Remove the backslash before the delimiter, as
the s command and GNU sed do.

---
 editors/sed.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/editors/sed.c
+++ b/editors/sed.c
@@ -407,7 +407,7 @@
 			delimiter = *++pos;
 		next = index_of_next_unescaped_regexp_delim(delimiter, ++pos);
 		if (next != 0) {
-			temp = copy_parsing_escapes(pos, next, 0);
+			temp = copy_parsing_escapes(pos, next, delimiter);
 			G.previous_regex_ptr = *regex = xzalloc(sizeof(regex_t));
 			xregcomp(*regex, temp, G.regex_type);
 			free(temp);
